id=a52sxq_soundtrigger_no_hal_reboot
name=Sound trigger: no audio HAL restart during calls; the resync restart runs after the call
author=kusti420
description=Hardening for the call audio report of 2026-10-22 ("I hear them for a second, then neither side hears anything", CS calls and WhatsApp, clean flash of the latest build). On the A52s vendor the sound trigger HAL lives inside the audio HAL process (android.hardware.audio.service), so every SoundTriggerHalEnforcer.reboot() (setprop sys.audio.restart.hal 1, audioserver.rc stops/starts the audio HAL) kills the audio HAL: a running call loses its voice/VoIP session in both directions, and audioserver restarts. That is the only mechanism seen so far on this ROM that kills all call audio at once. It is proven in the 9.3.62 tester log (logsrecentes.txt: "Exception caught from HAL, rebooting HAL" -> media.audio_flinger died), and calls are exactly when the HAL is pushed onto its non-LPI path ("lpi NOT supported due to concurrency"). The reboot is also what resyncs the middleware after a failed HAL call: stopRecognition marks the model PENDING_STOP before the HAL call and INACTIVE only on success, unloadSoundModel drops the model only on success, and SoundTriggerModule / Validation / SoundTriggerHelper above update only on success too. The HAL death after the reboot makes SoundTriggerModule.binderDied detach the old enforcer, attach a fresh one (empty model table) and send onModuleDied to every client (Validation marks the session DEAD, SoundTriggerHelper clears its model state, re-attaches, clients reload). The previous version of this patch never rebooted on a HAL status, so such a model could stay dead until the phone rebooted (part6 review M1). Now (K4StRetry, src in soundtrigger_load_no_reboot): (1) SoundTriggerHalEnforcer.handleException, the branch for anything that is not a RecoverableException or a dead HAL, calls K4StRetry.halError. (2) The 6 ModelCallbackEnforcer contract checks (event for an unknown model, recognitionStillActive with a non-success status, unload event for an unknown or active model) keep their wtfStack log and call K4StRetry.callbackError instead of reboot(); the event is dropped as in stock. Both decide by whether a call is going on: the AudioManager mode (OnModeChangedListener, registered when the HAL stack attaches, and AudioService.getMode() re-read), and, because a silent/vibrate incoming CS call keeps mode NORMAL (Telecom sets RINGTONE only when the ringer is audible) and some VoIP apps never set IN_COMMUNICATION, also the telephony call state of every active subscription (TelephonyCallback) and any active VOICE_COMMUNICATION / _SIGNALLING / NOTIFICATION_RINGTONE playback or VOICE_COMMUNICATION / VOICE_CALL / _UPLINK / _DOWNLINK recording (AudioManager callbacks). Those signals are cached from async callbacks on their own thread (K4StRetryState, which also does every registration with retry, every service lookup and the wakelock); the decision thread only reads cached state, so it makes no blocking binder call and takes no foreign lock, and the state thread never takes K4StRetry's lock or a middleware lock. A signal that is not known yet (registration failed, a subscription without its first state) counts as a call, within the existing bounds (45 s for a hung HAL call, 15 min for owed reboots); no telephony service at all (boot before the phone process) is no call. Mode/state: MODE_NORMAL -> log "Exception caught from HAL, rebooting HAL in 3000 ms unless a call starts" and reboot after the grace (see below). Any other mode (ringing, IN_CALL, IN_COMMUNICATION/VoIP, CALL_SCREENING, redirect) or the mode listener is not registered yet and AudioService says so (read directly) -> no reboot; a HalException goes to the client as RecoverableException(5, INTERNAL_ERROR); the reboot is owed and runs 30 s after the last call signal ended (cancelled if a call starts again, so a redial / call-back within half a minute never meets a restarting audio HAL; the cost is Hey Google / Bixby wake-up staying desynced ~25 s longer after such a call; skipped if that HAL stack died on its own meanwhile; owed errors and hung calls of one stack are settled by one reboot). Load refusals never reboot (soundtrigger_load_no_reboot). (3) SoundTriggerHalWatchdog deadline (a HAL call still running after 3 s; stock reboots at once, which is how it unblocks the caller): K4StRetry.watchdogExpired. Mode NORMAL (re-read from AudioService) or AudioService not published yet (boot, no call possible) -> stock reboot now. Call/VoIP/ringing -> no reboot, logged loudly with the caller's stack; the caller stays blocked in the HAL (only the sound trigger stack waits: DeviceStateHandler's executor, middleware/SoundTriggerHelper locks and their binder callers; audio policy's capture-state callback is oneway, checked in capture_state_listener-aidl-cpp.so: transact flags 1). Watchdog.close (hooked after the timer is cancelled) clears it when the HAL call returns late: no reboot then; a call returning right at the deadline is caught under the same lock (no stale entry). Still hung 30 s after the call ended -> reboot; hung for 45 s past the deadline during the call (48 s in all, below the system_server Watchdog's 60 s, so the blocked system_server threads can never get system_server killed mid-call) -> treated as a dead HAL, reboot; trade-off: a truly hung sound trigger HAL call costs the call its audio after ~48 s instead of 3 s (stock). NORMAL-mode HAL errors / callback violations now reboot after a 3 s grace instead of at once (mode listeners lag AudioService.setMode by ~0.2-0.5 s, and the HAL's call concurrency handling runs inside setMode), and the mode is re-read from AudioService before any reboot (getMode() is a lock-free field read on this ROM). The grace and the after-call wait hold a partial wakelock for delay + 1 s. While anything is owed, AudioService's own mode (not the listener's) and the call signals are re-checked every 60 s; NORMAL with no signal on two checks in a row runs the owed reboots, so a mode listener stuck on a call mode (a failed setPhoneState at hang-up) cannot keep them owed forever. Owed HAL-error reboots wait for as long as a call/VoIP mode lasts (AudioService drops a VoIP mode owner that neither plays nor records after 6 s). Reboots are decided on the K4StRetry thread, which never calls into the middleware. One reboot per HAL stack per resync. NOT proven to be the cause of the 2026-10-22 report. Logcat: grep K4StRetry ("not rebooting the HAL (shared with call audio)", "NOT rebooting the audio HAL", "no call: rebooting the sound trigger HAL now", "the hung sound trigger HAL call returned") and SoundTriggerHalEnforcer / SoundTriggerHalWatchdog.
